<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>1.7.3: Understanding Equations with No Solution or Infinitely Many</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00172</md:content-id>
    <md:title>1.7.3: Understanding Equations with No Solution or Infinitely Many</md:title>
    <md:uuid>2051a1d8-e872-4c53-9fcd-f52c36327c15</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity</title>
<para>Noah is having trouble solving two equations. In each case, he took steps that he thought were acceptable but ended
  up with statements that are clearly not true.</para>
<para>Analyze Noah’s work on each equation and the moves he made. Were they acceptable moves? Why do you think he
  ended up with a false equation?</para>
<para>Discuss your observations with your group and be prepared to share your conclusions. If you get stuck, consider
  solving each equation.</para>
<div class="os-raise-d-flex os-raise-justify-content-between">
  <para><emphasis>Example 1</emphasis>
    
    <emphasis>Step 1 - </emphasis>Original equation.
    \( x+6 =4x+1-3x \)
    
    <emphasis>Step 2 - </emphasis>Apply the Commutative Property.
    \( x+6 =4x-3x+1 \)
    
    <emphasis>Step 3 - </emphasis>Combine like terms.
    \( x+6 =x+1 \)
    
    <emphasis>Step 4 - </emphasis>Subtract \(x\) from each side.
    \( 6 =1 \)
  </para>
  <para><emphasis>Example 2</emphasis>
    
    <emphasis>Step 1 - </emphasis>Original equation.
    \( 2(5+x)-1=3x+9 \)
    
    <emphasis>Step 2 - </emphasis>Apply the Distributive Property.
    \( 10+2x-1 =3x+9 \)
    
    <emphasis>Step 3 - </emphasis>Subtract 10 from each side.
     \( 2x-1 =3x-1 \)
    
    <emphasis>Step 4 - </emphasis>Add 1 to each side.
    \( 2x=3x \)
  </para>
</div>

<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Why did Noah have no solution for either equation? </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para> Your answer may vary, but here is an example: 
      All the moves that Noah made were acceptable, but because he ended up with a false statement, that means there is no value of \(x\) that makes the first equation true. We can see it in the third line: \(x+6=x+1\). There is no number that can make the equation true.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Why did Noah arrive at no solution for the equation solved in Example 2?</para>
      </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para> Your answer may vary, but here is a sample. </para>
    <para>Example 1: All the moves that Noah made were acceptable, but because he ended up with a false statement, that
      means there is no value of \( x \) that makes the first equation true. We can see it in the third line: \( x + 6 =
      x + 1 \). There is no number that can make the equation true.</para>
    <para>Example 2: Noah's moves seem acceptable except for the last one. If he had subtracted \( 2x \) from each side of
      the equation, he would have \( 0 = x \). Instead, he divided both sides by \( x \) which would give an undefined
      number if \( x\;\;is\;\;0 \). </para>
    <para>Noah tried to solve a third equation and ended up with a true statement, without variables.</para>
    <para><emphasis>Step 1 - </emphasis>Original equation.</para>
    <para>\( 2 (4 + x) - 2 = 2x + 6 \)</para>
    <para><emphasis>Step 2 - </emphasis>Apply the Distributive Property.</para>
    <para>\( 8+ 2x - 2 =2x + 6 \)</para>
    <para><emphasis>Step 3 - </emphasis>Combine Like Terms.</para>
    <para>\( 2x +6 = 2x +6 \)</para>
    <para><emphasis>Step 4 - </emphasis>Subtract 6 from both sides.       </para>
    <para>\( 2x = 2x \)</para>
    <para><emphasis>Step 5 - </emphasis>Subtract \(2x\) from both sides.</para>
    <para>\( 0=0 \) </para>
    
    <para>Since there is not a variable and the statement is true, any value of \(x\) would be a solution for the original equation. So, this equation has infinitely many solutions.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
  
  </problem><solution>
  <para id="fs-id1168345252532">3. In middle school, you used tape diagrams to represent division. This tape diagram shows that 6÷2=3</para>
